Graduate theses must satisfy institutional layout constraints while managing bilingual metadata, long-form content, and several kinds of cross-reference. Deferring all formatting work until the final stage can therefore make writing and layout correction interfere with each other. This tutorial aims to establish a reproducible Typst workflow for graduate theses at Northeast Normal University (NENU), enabling first-time users to begin with a minimal compilable document and proceed through configuration, writing, assembly, and pre-submission verification.

The tutorial treats the repository's current config and info contracts as its boundary and follows a compile-first method: compile the project, configure its information, and then expand it chapter by chapter. It first explains the switches for thesis type, anonymization, and double-sided layout together with the bilingual information fields; it then organizes independent chapters and front and back matter with include. Next, it uses standard Typst elements, labels, and the template's bibliography entry point to handle equations, figures, tables, in-text citations, and references. Finally, it checks both the minimal example and the complete tutorial with Typst CLI commands that retain the project root and font path, followed by page-by-page inspection of the resulting PDF. The tutorial conducts no comparative performance experiment; its account is derived from the current implementation and compilable examples.

The resulting workflow assigns each responsibility to a clear layer: config selects behavior already implemented by the template, info supplies complete and correctly typed thesis data, chapter files hold independently maintainable content, and the template consistently generates front matter, catalogs, and chapter-based numbering and references for equations, figures, and tables. The bibliography module parses citations and produces the reference list. This arrangement automates stable, repetitive work while retaining human responsibility for reviewing titles, abstracts, subject information, figure and table content, and bibliographic data.

The tutorial concludes that following the public contract, keeping content modular, and compiling continuously provide a more maintainable thesis workflow than attempting to edit layout results near submission. Template automation cannot replace the latest requirements of the university and the relevant school, nor can it replace manual checks for anonymity, fonts, pagination, figures, tables, and references. Before submission, users should rerun both minimal and complete compilation checks and verify each item against the PDF actually produced.
